    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `complete_paragraph` in `app/tgi.py`. It creates an `InferenceClient(model=endpoint_url)` the same way `app/titles.py` does, then calls `text_generation` with:\n\n- `max_new_tokens=200`\n- `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]`: in the pinned huggingface-hub 2.0.0 the argument is called `stop`, not the older `stop_sequences`. I checked this in the installed library.\n- `return_full_text=False`: this makes sure the prompt isn't included in the output. It doesn't rely on the server's default.\n\nIt returns `generated.rstrip()`. TGI includes the matched `\"\\n\\n\"` at the end of the generated text, so the `rstrip()` also removes that.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes (1 test), but that test only checks that the module imports. To check the actual request, I faked the HTTP call and ran the function against it. The client posted exactly this to the endpoint URL:\n```\n{'inputs': 'Once upon a time', 'parameters': {'max_new_tokens': 200, 'return_full_text': False, 'stop': ['\\n\\n']}}\n```\nA fake response of `\" a sentence.\\n\\n\"` came back as `' a sentence.'`. I haven't run it against a real TGI server.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my attempt to delete the throwaway script `_check_tgi.py` in the project root needed approval, so it's still there. You can delete it, or tell me to and I'll remove it. I didn't add any dependencies."
